<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle kVar(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}_{1}})+(n-k)Var(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}_{2}})+k(\mu_{1}-\mu)^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\mu)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle kVar(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}_{1}})+(n-k)Var(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}_{2}})+k(\mu_{1}-\mu)^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\mu)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting this into the upper bound, we get</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p29" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex71"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&lt;\frac{nVar(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}})-\left(kVar(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}_{1}})+(n-k)Var(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}_{2}})\right)}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&lt;\frac{nVar(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}})-\left(kVar(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}_{1}})+(n-k)Var(\tilde{\boldsymbol{A}_{2}})\right)}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex72"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{k(\mu_{1}-\mu)^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\mu)^{2}}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{k(\mu_{1}-\mu)^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\mu)^{2}}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex73"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&lt;\max_{1\leq k&lt;n}\frac{k(\mu_{1}-\mu)^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\mu)^{2}}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&lt;\max_{1\leq k&lt;n}\frac{k(\mu_{1}-\mu)^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\mu)^{2}}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p30.1" class="ltx_p">We can further simplify the numerator.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p31" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex74"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k(\mu_{1}-\mu)^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\mu)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k(\mu_{1}-\mu)^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\mu)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex75"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex75.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k(\mu_{1}-\frac{k\mu_{1}+(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\frac{k\mu_{1}+(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k(\mu_{1}-\frac{k\mu_{1}+(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}+(n-k)(\mu_{2}-\frac{k\mu_{1}+(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex76"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex76.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k(\frac{n\mu_{1}-k\mu_{1}-(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}+(n-k)(\frac{n\mu_{2}-k\mu_{1}-(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k(\frac{n\mu_{1}-k\mu_{1}-(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}+(n-k)(\frac{n\mu_{2}-k\mu_{1}-(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex77"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex77.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k(\frac{(n-k)\mu_{1}-(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}+(n-k)(\frac{-k\mu_{1}+k\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k(\frac{(n-k)\mu_{1}-(n-k)\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}+(n-k)(\frac{-k\mu_{1}+k\mu_{2}}{n})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex78"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex78.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k(\frac{(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})}{n})^{2}+(n-k)(\frac{-k(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})}{n})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k(\frac{(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})}{n})^{2}+(n-k)(\frac{-k(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})}{n})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex79"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex79.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{k(n-k)^{2}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}+(n-k)k^{2}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{n^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{k(n-k)^{2}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}+(n-k)k^{2}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{n^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex80"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex80.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{kn-k^{2}+k^{2}}{n^{2}}(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{kn-k^{2}+k^{2}}{n^{2}}(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex81"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex81.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{k}{n}(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{k}{n}(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p32.1" class="ltx_p">So the upper bound is</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p33" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex82"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda&lt;\max_{1\leq k&lt;n}\frac{\frac{k}{n}(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda&lt;\max_{1\leq k&lt;n}\frac{\frac{k}{n}(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p34.1" class="ltx_p">To balance efficiency and accuracy, we avoid approaching the precise upper bound, as it maximizes efficiency at the cost of minimal accuracy. Instead, a faster approximation is employed to estimate the upper bound. Using <math id="A3.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="A3.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex83"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex83.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&lt;\frac{\frac{\frac{n}{2}}{n}(n-\frac{n}{2})(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{\frac{n}{\frac{n}{2}}+\frac{n}{n-\frac{n}{2}}-1}&lt;\max_{1\leq k&lt;n}\frac{\frac{k}{n}(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&lt;\frac{\frac{\frac{n}{2}}{n}(n-\frac{n}{2})(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{\frac{n}{\frac{n}{2}}+\frac{n}{n-\frac{n}{2}}-1}&lt;\max_{1\leq k&lt;n}\frac{\frac{k}{n}(n-k)(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{\frac{n}{k}+\frac{n}{n-k}-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex84"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex84.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&lt;\frac{\frac{1}{2}(\frac{n}{2})(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{2+\frac{n}{\frac{n}{2}}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&lt;\frac{\frac{1}{2}(\frac{n}{2})(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{2+\frac{n}{\frac{n}{2}}-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex85"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\frac{n}{4}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\frac{n}{4}(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex86"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{n(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{n(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{12}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p35.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we have</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p36" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex87"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{min}\approx\frac{(|a_{1}|-|a_{2}|)^{2}}{3n}&lt;\lambda&lt;\frac{n(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{12}\approx\lambda_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{min}\approx\frac{(|a_{1}|-|a_{2}|)^{2}}{3n}&lt;\lambda&lt;\frac{n(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{12}\approx\lambda_{max}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p37.1" class="ltx_p">And we can control <math id="A3.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="A3.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\lambda}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\lambda}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p38" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A3.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex88"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex88.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Lambda(\tilde{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Lambda(\tilde{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex89"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda_{min}+\tilde{\lambda}(\lambda_{max}-\lambda_{min})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda_{min}+\tilde{\lambda}(\lambda_{max}-\lambda_{min})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex90"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{(|a_{1}|-|a_{2}|)^{2}}{3n}+\tilde{\lambda}(\frac{n(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{12}-\frac{(|a_{1}|-|a_{2}|)^{2}}{3n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{(|a_{1}|-|a_{2}|)^{2}}{3n}+\tilde{\lambda}(\frac{n(\mu_{1}-\mu_{2})^{2}}{12}-\frac{(|a_{1}|-|a_{2}|)^{2}}{3n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p39.1" class="ltx_p">For the optimal <math id="A3.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, the hypothesis is that a suitable value of <math id="A3.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> should lie within the latter half of the range, but not too close to the upper bound, which prioritizes efficiency over accuracy. A precise choice of <math id="A3.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> leads to a specific optimal max group, which can be identified via binary search over the result max group of Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Since this process is too computationally intensive, we hypothesize that <math id="A3.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\lambda}^{\ast}\approx 0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\lambda}^{\ast}\approx 0.75</annotation></semantics></math> provides a reasonable approximation.
Empirical results on Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, however, indicate that <math id="A3.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> has negligible impact on quantization performance of PPL. The same holds for <math id="A3.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding table is omitted, as it is virtually identical to Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2.4 Hyperparameters. ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. This observation is theoretically supported: <math id="A3.p39.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is originally introduced to determine the optimal number of groups in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, whereas in other algorithms, the number of groups is treated as a user-defined hyperparameter, rendering it inapplicable. For consistency of notation and to preserve the theoretical formulation of Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we keep the <math id="A3.p39.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> term throughout.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Supplementary Experiments</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Additional experiments setup</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the main text shows that weight MSE is proportional with downstream quantization quality, we use MSE as a proxy in controlled ablations. we evaluate the proposed algorithms on synthetic matrices sampled from <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and run all experiments on a single CPU. These standardized test cases allow us to compare quantization loss, runtime, and hyperparameter sensitivity using MSE as a proxy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Comparison for quantization loss</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the three core variants (Algorithms <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) by proxy MSE on synthetic square matrices. Figures <a href="#A4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ D.2 Comparison for quantization loss ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#A4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ D.2 Comparison for quantization loss ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compare against <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">XNOR</span>, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Blocked-XNOR</span>, and an all-zero dummy baseline. As expected, the all-zero dummy yields the largest error, while <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">XNOR</span> and <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Blocked-XNOR</span> produce moderate MSE. In contrast, our methods consistently reduce MSE to near zero on these controlled instances.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#A4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ D.2 Comparison for quantization loss ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> eventually matches <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">XNOR</span>. This is an implementation artifact of the dynamic window schedule: when the window size grows to <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix dimension, windowed merging degenerates to standard <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">XNOR</span>. For sufficiently large inputs, the code increases the window automatically, causing this convergence behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#A4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ D.2 Comparison for quantization loss ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we omit Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> due to prohibitive runtime at larger sizes. We evaluate square matrices with side length <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{2,4,\dots,2048\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{2,4,\dots,2048\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> grows, the loss curves become smoother analogs of the small-scale behavior, suggesting that the qualitative trends generalize. Moreover, Algorithms <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> closely track the optimum provided by Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, indicating that their approximation gap is negligible in terms of MSE on these instances. The relative ordering of errors typically follows our expectation (DG <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math> GG <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math> WGM), but the absolute differences remain small.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We omit Algorithm 4 (<span id="A4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WGM-LO</span>) from this proxy ablation because the goal here is to benchmark heuristic grouping strategies against the dynamic-programming oracle (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> already achieves MSE nearly indistinguishable from the oracle on these controlled instances, establishing that its approximation gap is negligible in the proxy regime. In contrast, <span id="A4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WGM-LO</span> is a local refinement applied on top of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and it is feasible to run to completion on LLMs, we therefore evaluate <span id="A4.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WGM-LO</span> directly against Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> using full PPL/QA rather than the MSE proxy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2509.03054v3/25n_to_loss.png" id="A4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/119;" width="238" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F3.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="A4.F3.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Algorithm comparison for small matrix, quantization loss against matrix sized <math id="A4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2509.03054v3/2048n_to_loss.png" id="A4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/119;" width="238" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F3.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="A4.F3.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Algorithm comparison for large matrix, quantization loss against matrix sized <math id="A4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Comparison for quantization speed</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the setup in the previous section, we replace the MSE proxy with wall-clock quantization time and compare runtime on both small and large square matrices. In Figure <a href="#A4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ D.3 Comparison for quantization speed ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">XNOR</span> and <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Blocked-XNOR</span> are the fastest baselines, as they rely on lightweight mean/sign computations. Among our methods, Algorithms <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> are substantially faster than the oracle Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, consistent with the fact that Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> trades increased computational complexity for optimality.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The abrupt runtime change observed for Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is due to its dynamic window schedule: once the window size grows to the matrix dimension <math id="A4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the method degenerates to standard <span id="A4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">XNOR</span>, leading to a sudden speed-up.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As the matrix size increases, runtime grows rapidly and Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> becomes impractical. Figure <a href="#A4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ D.3 Comparison for quantization speed ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the same comparison on larger matrices. Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> can require on the order of <math id="A4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> seconds at the largest size, whereas Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> completes within <math id="A4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 10</annotation></semantics></math> seconds, i.e., over two orders of magnitude faster. <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">XNOR</span> and <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Blocked-XNOR</span> remain the fastest, but their advantage narrows as the matrix size grows. Taken together with its near-oracle loss in the proxy study, Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> offers the best accuracy–efficiency trade-off and is therefore used as the default variant in subsequent experiments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2509.03054v3/25n_to_speed.png" id="A4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/119;" width="238" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F5.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A4.F5.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Algorithm comparison for small matrix, time used for quantization on CPU against matrix sized <math id="A4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F5.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2509.03054v3/2048n_to_speed.png" id="A4.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/119;" width="238" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F5.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="A4.F5.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Algorithm comparison for large matrix, time used for quantization on CPU against matrix sized <math id="A4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Comparison for loss against <math id="A4.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for greedy algorithm and windowed version</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ D.4 Comparison for loss against 𝜆 for greedy algorithm and windowed version ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports the MSE against <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> on a synthetic matrix in <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{512\times 512}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{512\times 512}</annotation></semantics></math>, chosen as a compromise between computational cost and performance. We evaluate only Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, since Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is not computationally feasible at this scale and Algorithm <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is a modified Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Varying <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> has limited practical effect for Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where the targeted number of groups is specified externally rather than optimized through <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, both methods attain their best MSE at <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows negligible variation and no general trends across the entire range of <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, mirroring the weak dependence observed in downstream evaluations in the main text. These results suggest that, outside Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> does not serve as an effective control knob for trading performance and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03054v3/lambda_to_loss.png" id="A4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Loss against <math id="A4.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for greedy algorithm and windowed version.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.5 </span>Comparison for max groups against MSE and speed</h3>

<div id="A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since we need to choose the max group manually, we evaluate its impact on quantization error and speed using matrix <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\mathbb{R}^{512\times 512}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\mathbb{R}^{512\times 512}</annotation></semantics></math> to discover the best operating bit length in Figure <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ D.5 Comparison for max groups against MSE and speed ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ D.5 Comparison for max groups against MSE and speed ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. As shown in the figures, the MSE for Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Remaining Three Algorithms ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> improves and then stabilizes, plateaus around 32 groups, while quantization time decreases slowly as the max group increases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F8.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2509.03054v3/131072g_to_loss.png" id="A4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/119;" width="238" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F8.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A4.F8.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Max groups against MSE.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F8.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2509.03054v3/131072g_to_speed.png" id="A4.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/119;" width="238" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F8.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A4.F8.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Max groups against quantization speed.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.6 </span>Comparison for window (initial group) size against quantization loss and speed.</h3>

<div id="A4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, the impact of different window sizes in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 3.3.3 Algorithm , Windowed Greedy Merging ‣ 3.3 Four Tailored Algorithms ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> on MSE and quantization speed was evaluated in the matrix simulation experiments, as shown in Figure <a href="#A4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ D.6 Comparison for window (initial group) size against quantization loss and speed. ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#A4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ D.6 Comparison for window (initial group) size against quantization loss and speed. ‣ Appendix D Supplementary Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. It was observed that increasing the window size led to higher MSE and reduced quantization time. When the window size was below 64, the MSE remained near its minimum, while the speed improvement flattened out between 64 and 1024. Therefore, a window size of 64 is the best balance point.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F10.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2509.03054v3/2048w_to_loss.png" id="A4.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/119;" width="238" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F10.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A4.F10.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Window (initial group) size against loss.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F10.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><img src="2509.03054v3/2048w_to_speed.png" id="A4.F10.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/119;" width="238" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F10.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A4.F10.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Window (initial group) size against quantization speed.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Expanded tables</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For completeness, we provide full versions of several condensed tables from the main text. Specifically, Table <a href="#S4.T7.st2" title="Table 7(b) ‣ Table 7 ‣ 4.2.4 Hyperparameters. ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a> is expanded into Tables <a href="#A5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix E Expanded tables ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix E Expanded tables ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>; Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.2.4 Hyperparameters. ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is expanded into Tables <a href="#A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix E Expanded tables ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and <a href="#A5.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Appendix E Expanded tables ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>; and Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2.4 Hyperparameters. ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is expanded into Table <a href="#A5.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Appendix E Expanded tables ‣ Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. The qualitative trends are consistent with the condensed results in the main text.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T8" class="ltx_table">
